[4] Euclidean zero-curvature regular tilings
  triangular tiling                cfg=(3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3)   kappa=     0 V=flat/negative faces={}                     role=flat six-triangle vertex
  square tiling                    cfg=(4, 4, 4, 4)         kappa=     0 V=flat/negative faces={}                     role=flat self-dual square grid
  hexagonal tiling                 cfg=(6, 6, 6)            kappa=     0 V=flat/negative faces={}                     role=flat dual of triangular grid

[5] Prompt recursion counts for the three Euclidean regular tilings
  square self n^2                  n=2:4, n=3:9, n=4:16, n=5:25, n=6:36, n=7:49
  triangle self 4^k                k=1:4, k=2:16, k=3:64, k=4:256, k=5:1024
  triangle->hex exchange 6*n^2     n=1:6, n=2:24, n=3:54, n=4:96, n=5:150
  hex centered patch 1+3r(r+1)     r=1:7, r=2:19, r=3:37, r=4:61, r=5:91, r=6:127
  hex self hexaflake 7^k           k=1:7, k=2:49, k=3:343, k=4:2401
  readout:
    square recursion is n^2/self-dual; triangle self-recursion is dyadic
    4^k; triangle<->hex exchange is 6*n^2; hex self-recursion has two
    useful readings: centered lattice patches 1+3r(r+1), and hexaflake
    self-similar packets 7^k.

[6] Johnson-solid guardrail: finite nonuniform defect packets
  square pyramid J1:
    apex         mult=1  cfg=(3, 3, 3, 3)     kappa=1/3 contribution=1/3
    base         mult=4  cfg=(3, 3, 4)        kappa=5/12 contribution=5/3
    total_curvature=2
  pentagonal pyramid J2:
    apex         mult=1  cfg=(3, 3, 3, 3, 3)  kappa=1/6 contribution=1/6
    base         mult=5  cfg=(3, 3, 5)        kappa=11/30 contribution=11/6
    total_curvature=2
  triangular prism as Johnson-like check:
    all vertices mult=6  cfg=(3, 4, 4)        kappa=1/3 contribution=2
    total_curvature=2
  readout:
    Johnson solids should be treated like finite residual atlases.  They
    preserve regular-face local defects, but not a single transitive
    vertex configuration or global recursion law.

[7] Prism / antiprism annular families
  3-gonal prism          cfg=(4, 4, 3)        kappa=  1/3 V=  6
  3-gonal antiprism      cfg=(3, 3, 3, 3)     kappa=  1/3 V=  6
  4-gonal prism          cfg=(4, 4, 4)        kappa=  1/4 V=  8
  4-gonal antiprism      cfg=(3, 3, 3, 4)     kappa=  1/4 V=  8
  5-gonal prism          cfg=(4, 4, 5)        kappa=  1/5 V= 10
  5-gonal antiprism      cfg=(3, 3, 3, 5)     kappa=  1/5 V= 10
  6-gonal prism          cfg=(4, 4, 6)        kappa=  1/6 V= 12
  6-gonal antiprism      cfg=(3, 3, 3, 6)     kappa=  1/6 V= 12
  7-gonal prism          cfg=(4, 4, 7)        kappa=  1/7 V= 14  <-- 14 vertices / seven-sector defect
  7-gonal antiprism      cfg=(3, 3, 3, 7)     kappa=  1/7 V= 14  <-- 14 vertices / seven-sector defect
  8-gonal prism          cfg=(4, 4, 8)        kappa=  1/8 V= 16
  8-gonal antiprism      cfg=(3, 3, 3, 8)     kappa=  1/8 V= 16
  9-gonal prism          cfg=(4, 4, 9)        kappa=  1/9 V= 18
  9-gonal antiprism      cfg=(3, 3, 3, 9)     kappa=  1/9 V= 18
  10-gonal prism         cfg=(4, 4, 10)       kappa= 1/10 V= 20
  10-gonal antiprism     cfg=(3, 3, 3, 10)    kappa= 1/10 V= 20
  11-gonal prism         cfg=(4, 4, 11)       kappa= 1/11 V= 22
  11-gonal antiprism     cfg=(3, 3, 3, 11)    kappa= 1/11 V= 22
  12-gonal prism         cfg=(4, 4, 12)       kappa= 1/12 V= 24
  12-gonal antiprism     cfg=(3, 3, 3, 12)    kappa= 1/12 V= 24
  13-gonal prism         cfg=(4, 4, 13)       kappa= 1/13 V= 26
  13-gonal antiprism     cfg=(3, 3, 3, 13)    kappa= 1/13 V= 26
  14-gonal prism         cfg=(4, 4, 14)       kappa= 1/14 V= 28  <-- 28 vertices / LRC 1/14 defect
  14-gonal antiprism     cfg=(3, 3, 3, 14)    kappa= 1/14 V= 28  <-- 28 vertices / LRC 1/14 defect
  15-gonal prism         cfg=(4, 4, 15)       kappa= 1/15 V= 30
  15-gonal antiprism     cfg=(3, 3, 3, 15)    kappa= 1/15 V= 30
  16-gonal prism         cfg=(4, 4, 16)       kappa= 1/16 V= 32
  16-gonal antiprism     cfg=(3, 3, 3, 16)    kappa= 1/16 V= 32
  readout:
    n-gonal prisms (4,4,n) and antiprisms (3,3,3,n) always have V=2n
    and per-vertex kappa=1/n.  Thus n=14 gives a 28-point annular
    carrier at exactly the LRC 1/14 scale, complementary to the q=3
    unital's 28-point pair-incidence carrier.

[8] Uniform configurations matching the LRC 14/28 or 1/7,1/14 scales
  cfg=(3, 3, 3, 7)       kappa=  1/7 V= 14 faces={3:14, 7:2}
  cfg=(4, 4, 7)          kappa=  1/7 V= 14 faces={4:7, 7:2}
  cfg=(3, 3, 3, 14)      kappa= 1/14 V= 28 faces={3:28, 14:2}
  cfg=(4, 4, 14)         kappa= 1/14 V= 28 faces={4:14, 14:2}
  readout:
    The clean uniform matches are exactly the prism/antiprism annuli.
    That makes the 14/28 carrier cyclic and branch-local, unlike the
    q=3 unital which is pair-unique but not cyclic.

[9] Proposed LRC14 carrier split
  q=3 unital:
    preserves pair incidence; 28 points; detects repeated pairs such as
    the H12 conflict in HYP-2942.
  14-gonal prism/antiprism:
    preserves cyclic annular order; 28 vertices; per-vertex defect 1/14;
    candidate carrier for apex clocks, two 14-cycles, and half-step twists.
  Euclidean tilings:
    preserve zero-defect recursion rules; useful as flat limits and
    self/dual recursion mnemonics, not as finite LRC certificates.
  Johnson solids:
    preserve finite nonuniform defect packets; useful as finite residual
    atlases after uniform/prism carriers fail.

